[0mThe ablation study examined how different roles affect the final results. Two tasks were run, involving generating effective code and calculating average statistics. When certain roles were excluded, the system produced unworkable code.

**Results by role configuration (Table 3):**

| Roles | # Agents | # Lines | Expense | Revisions | Executability |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Engineer only | 1 | 83.0 | $0.915 | 10 | 1.0 |
| + Product manager | 2 | 112.0 | $1.059 | 6.5 | 2.0 |
| + Product manager + Architect | 3 | 143.0 | $1.204 | 4.0 | 2.5 |
| + Product manager + Project manager | 3 | 205.0 | $1.251 | 3.5 | 2.0 |
| All four roles | 4 | 191.0 | $1.385 | 2.5 | 4.0 |

**Key findings:**
- Adding roles beyond the Engineer consistently improved both human revision cost and executability.
- Revisions fell from 10 with the Engineer alone to 2.5 with all four roles.
- Executability rose from 1.0 to 4.0, the maximum score.
- More roles raised expenses slightly, from $0.915 to $1.385. Overall performance still improved noticeably, which shows the value of the different roles.
